/// \defgroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops Int<64> members kept as separate retail functions
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief The default constructor, five arithmetic operators, a
///        comparison, a remainder and a shift operator, bounded
///        randomization, DER decoding, the bit count and the primality test
///        of the Int big-integer template, instantiated for Int<64>
///        (BigInt).
///
/// Int itself, its number format and its shared static members are
/// described on the Int class page in \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath. Every
/// member in this group is a one-line inline function in int.h. It forwards
/// to one XMP_ routine in mpmath.cpp, passing the class's digit count of 64,
/// and some members also store that routine's status in one of Int's
/// static members. Each entry names the routine it calls and the static
/// member it writes, if any. The retail copies call the same routines.
///
/// The operators are named here as int.h declares them: operator+=
/// taking an Int, operator+ and operator- taking an unsigned short, the
/// two operator* overloads, operator> taking an Int, the remainder
/// operator taking an unsigned short, and operator<< taking an int.
///
/// \par Where they are used
/// In BFME 2's own source, these members are used by PKey's methods in
/// pk.cpp and by two other int.h templates, Generate_Prime and
/// RemainderTable. INIClass::Get_PKey reaches Int::DERDecode and
/// Int::BitCount only through PKey::Decode_Exponent and
/// PKey::Decode_Modulus. Each entry names the users of its member. In
/// retail, every one of those users compiles the member inline and calls
/// the XMP_ routine directly. Retail also keeps a separate copy of each
/// member listed here, but a scan of the retail image finds no call, jump
/// or stored address that targets any of those copies. The users named in
/// these entries are therefore users in the source, not callers of the
/// separate copies.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here is data-driven. No game INI field, map script, Apt movie
/// or asset reaches this code, and no game code is known to call it at all
/// (see \ref grp_w1_wwlib_pkey_mpmath for that evidence and its limits). A
/// change here cannot affect lockstep sync, CRC checks, saves or replays
/// while that holds.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - A port can leave the separate copies out without losing anything.
///   Changing a member's body in int.h is a different matter: it also
///   changes every inline use of it in Generate_Prime, RemainderTable and
///   PKey.
/// - operator+= and operator+ write Int's static Carry, operator- writes
///   Borrow, both operator* overloads write Error, and the remainder
///   operator writes Remainder. All four statics are shared by every
///   Int<64>. These members compute the values they return in this object
///   or in a local, and those values do not depend on the statics, but two
///   threads using them at once overwrite each other's Carry, Borrow,
///   Error and Remainder (a data race), so those values cannot be trusted.
///   Neither can the result of a member that uses Remainder as working
///   storage, such as operator*=, which is not in this group. A port that
///   needs thread safety can make the statics per-thread or return the
///   status values instead.
/// - operator+, operator- and both operator* overloads build their result
///   in a 2,048-bit (256-byte) temporary that the default constructor
///   zeroes first, and return it by value. operator<< copies this value
///   into its temporary instead.

/// \fn Int::Int(void)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Constructs the number zero.
///
/// Calls XMP_Init with a value of 0, which clears all 64 digits. Int's
/// other members that build a new number start from this constructor.
/// They include operator+, operator- and both operator* overloads in this
/// group, and Int::exp_b_mod_c and Int::Inverse. So does Generate_Prime
/// for its q and p, and PKey::Decrypt for its block buffer. The startup
/// initializer of Int<64>'s static Remainder also calls XMP_Init with
/// zero, the inlined form of this constructor.
///
/// Int has no destructor, copy constructor or assignment operator of its
/// own, so a copy is a plain copy of the digit array.
/// \see XMP_Init

/// \fn Int & Int::operator+=(const Int &number)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Adds number to this value in place and records the carry.
///
/// Calls XMP_Add with this number as both the output and the left operand
/// and no carry in. The carry out of the top digit goes to the static
/// Carry. In two's complement, addition is the same for signed and
/// unsigned values. The sum wraps at 2,048 bits.
///
/// Generate_Prime steps its candidates with it, q by 2 and p by 4. The
/// small integer is converted to an Int by Int's unsigned long constructor
/// first.
/// \param number The value added. It may be this same object, because
///               XMP_Add reads each digit before it writes the result.
/// \return This object.
/// \see XMP_Add, Generate_Prime

/// \fn Int Int::operator+(unsigned short b) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns this value plus a 16-bit unsigned value, and records the
///        carry.
///
/// Calls XMP_Add_Int into a new zeroed result, with no carry in, and
/// stores the carry out of the top digit in the static Carry. b is widened
/// to a full 32-bit digit and added to the lowest digit.
///
/// Generate_Prime uses it to form p = 2q + 1 from its candidate q.
/// \param b The value added, 0 to 65535.
/// \return The sum, wrapped to 2,048 bits.
/// \see XMP_Add_Int, Generate_Prime

/// \fn Int Int::operator-(unsigned short b) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns this value minus a 16-bit unsigned value, and records the
///        borrow.
///
/// Calls XMP_Sub_Int into a new zeroed result, with no borrow in, and
/// stores the borrow out of the top digit in the static Borrow.
///
/// Generate_Prime computes the upper bound of its q range with it, as
/// 1 shifted left by pbits - 1, minus 1. PKey::Generate uses it to form
/// (p - 1) * (q - 1), the modulus that Int::Inverse works in when it
/// derives the private exponent.
/// \param b The value subtracted, 0 to 65535.
/// \return The difference, wrapped to 2,048 bits. Subtracting from a
///         smaller value gives a negative number in two's complement.
/// \see XMP_Sub_Int, PKey::Generate

/// \fn Int Int::operator*(unsigned short b) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns this value multiplied by a 16-bit unsigned value.
///
/// Calls XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int into a new zeroed result and stores its
/// return value in the static Error. That routine always returns 0, so the
/// effect is to clear Error. The product keeps the low 2,048 bits, and
/// overflow is not reported.
///
/// Generate_Prime doubles q with it. It writes q*2 with a plain int 2,
/// and overload resolution picks this operator rather than the one that
/// takes an Int: converting int to unsigned short is a standard
/// conversion, while reaching Int needs the converting constructor. The
/// retail Generate_Prime calls XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int at that point, which
/// agrees.
/// \param b The multiplier. See the warning.
/// \return The product.
/// \warning XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int takes the multiplier as a signed short,
///          so a b of 32768 or more arrives negative and the product can
///          come out wrong (see XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int). Generate_Prime
///          passes 2.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Mult_Int, Generate_Prime

/// \fn Int Int::operator*(const Int &multiplier) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns the signed product of this value and multiplier.
///
/// Calls XMP_Signed_Mult into a new zeroed result and stores its return
/// value, always 0, in the static Error. XMP_Signed_Mult multiplies the
/// magnitudes and negates the product when the signs differ. The product
/// keeps the low 2,048 bits, and overflow is not reported. The result is a
/// fresh object, so it never overlaps an operand, which XMP_Signed_Mult
/// requires. Int's operator*= is the one that works around that by copying
/// into the static Remainder first; it is not part of this group.
///
/// PKey::Generate computes the modulus p * q and the product
/// (p - 1) * (q - 1) with it.
/// \param multiplier The second factor.
/// \return The product.
/// \see XMP_Signed_Mult, PKey::Generate

/// \fn int Int::operator>(const Int &number) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Reports whether this value is greater than number, compared as
///        unsigned values.
///
/// Returns whether XMP_Compare gives a positive result. XMP_Compare treats
/// both numbers as unsigned, so although Int holds two's-complement values,
/// a negative number compares greater than every non-negative one.
///
/// Generate_Prime uses it to see whether stepping its candidate q has run
/// past the top of the q range, both to leave the search loop and to
/// decide whether to draw a new q.
/// \param number The right-hand value.
/// \return 1 if this value is greater when both are read as unsigned,
///         otherwise 0. The return type is int, not bool.
/// \see XMP_Compare, Generate_Prime

/// \fn unsigned short Int::operator%(unsigned short divisor) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns this value modulo a 16-bit divisor, and leaves the
///        quotient in the static Remainder.
///
/// Calls XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int with the static Remainder as the quotient
/// buffer and returns that routine's result, the remainder. Despite its
/// name, Remainder holds the quotient after this call. The dividend is read
/// as unsigned. Error is not touched.
///
/// RemainderTable's constructor calls it once for each prime in
/// mpmath.cpp's table, to record the starting number's remainder modulo
/// that prime.
/// \param divisor The divisor, 1 to 32768 (see XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int). A
///                zero divisor returns 0 and leaves Remainder unchanged.
/// \return The remainder, from 0 to divisor - 1.
/// \warning It overwrites the static Remainder, which every Int<64>
///          shares. A value kept there by another operation is lost.
///          Applied to Remainder itself, it returns 0, because
///          XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int clears the quotient buffer before it
///          reads the dividend.
/// \see XMP_Unsigned_Div_Int, RemainderTable

/// \fn Int Int::operator<<(int bits) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns a copy of this value shifted left by bits, filling with
///        zeros.
///
/// Copies this number into the result and shifts the copy with
/// XMP_Shift_Left_Bits. Bits shifted past bit 2,047 are lost, and nothing
/// reports it. No static member is written.
///
/// Generate_Prime builds the bounds of its q range with it, shifting 1
/// left by pbits - 2 for the lower bound and by pbits - 1 for the upper
/// bound. Its shift count is an unsigned short, promoted to int.
/// \param bits The shift count, 0 or more.
/// \return The shifted copy.
/// \warning In a 64-digit number, XMP_Shift_Left_Bits can give a wrong
///          result for a count that is a multiple of 32 from 64 to 1,984
///          (see XMP_Shift_Left_Bits for why, and for why larger counts
///          are not affected). Shifting 1 left by 64, for example, sets
///          bits 64 and 96. Shifting 1 left by any of those counts comes out
///          wrong, so Generate_Prime gets a wrong bound when pbits - 2 or
///          pbits - 1 is one of them: pbits of 65 or 66, 97 or 98, and
///          each pair 32 higher, up to 1,985 and 1,986. With pbits = 66
///          the lower bound comes out above the upper bound.
///          Nothing in retail is known to call PKey::Generate, which passes
///          its bit count to Generate_Prime unchanged.
/// \see XMP_Shift_Left_Bits, Generate_Prime

/// \fn void Int::Randomize(Straw &rng, const Int &minval, const Int &maxval)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Sets this value to a random odd number in the range minval to
///        maxval.
///
/// Calls XMP_Randomize_Bounded, which draws a uniformly distributed number
/// from minval to maxval, both included, then sets the lowest bit so the
/// result is odd. No static member is written.
///
/// Generate_Prime draws the starting q of each prime search with it. Its
/// upper bound, 2 to the power pbits - 1, minus 1, is odd, so setting the
/// low bit keeps q inside the range there.
/// \param rng    Source of the random bytes. Any key generated from the
///               result is only as unpredictable as these bytes.
/// \param minval The smallest value drawn.
/// \param maxval The largest value drawn. Must not be below minval.
/// \note When maxval is even and the draw equals it, setting the low bit
///       gives maxval + 1, one above the range. The result is never below
///       minval.
/// \see XMP_Randomize_Bounded, Generate_Prime

/// \fn void Int::DERDecode(const unsigned char *input)
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Loads this value from a DER-encoded INTEGER element.
///
/// Calls XMP_DER_Decode. There is no return value. If the element is not
/// an INTEGER, its long-form length uses more than two length bytes, or
/// the number is longer than 256 bytes, the value is left as it was and
/// nothing reports it. An accepted number is sign-extended.
///
/// PKey's constructor that takes two DER buffers decodes the exponent and
/// the modulus with it, and PKey::Decode_Modulus and PKey::Decode_Exponent
/// each decode one number. INIClass::Get_PKey reaches it through those two.
/// \param input The DER element. Not null. Its length is read from the
///              element, so the buffer must hold the whole element.
/// \note The encoder is not symmetric: Int's DER encoder, built on
///       XMP_DER_Encode, writes only the low 512 bits of a number, while
///       this decoder accepts numbers up to 2,048 bits. See the
///       mpmath.cpp file notes.
/// \see XMP_DER_Decode, XMP_DER_Encode, PKey::Decode_Modulus

/// \fn int Int::BitCount(void) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Returns the number of significant bits: the position of the
///        highest set bit plus one.
///
/// Calls XMP_Count_Bits. The number is read as unsigned, so any negative
/// value reports the full 2,048 bits.
///
/// PKey sets a key's bit precision to its modulus's bit count minus one.
/// It does so in its DER-decoding constructor, PKey::Decode_Modulus and
/// PKey::Generate, and that precision fixes the key's block sizes.
/// \return 0 for zero, up to 2,048.
/// \see XMP_Count_Bits, PKey::Plain_Block_Size

/// \fn bool Int::IsPrime(void) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1020_wwlib_bigint_template_ops
/// \brief Tests whether this value is probably prime.
///
/// Calls XMP_Is_Prime, which rejects even numbers, divides by every prime
/// up to 32719 and then runs a Fermat test to bases 2 and 3. A number that
/// passes is only probably prime; XMP_Is_Prime describes the steps and
/// their limits.
///
/// Generate_Prime tests its candidate q with it, and tests p = 2q + 1 only
/// when q passes.
/// \return true if the number is probably prime. false if it is composite,
///         and also for the odd primes up to 32719, as the XMP_Is_Prime
///         entry explains.
/// \see XMP_Is_Prime, Generate_Prime
